n$$\n6{{field-4}}{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =({{field-6}})^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $6.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." :question/worked-solution "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,6∤{n}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n6∤{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =(0)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $6.$ \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e48ea1-2219-5954-8101-def7c9c2be2c" :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99f4d7f-0008-5d7e-a1e0-3b6e685a8bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412562} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bfd400-05fc-5c55-9958-4138ce1b733b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412567} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e10b1-5d53-5cc3-b5d0-f4f7f58fe3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 