/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412537} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a09f6-d3ec-587e-96c8-61dae52f34b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412555} {:answer/id #uuid "f62648ee-9402-5164-8e8a-bc4ac557844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62648ee-9402-5164-8e8a-bc4ac557844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6bb26c4-97c0-5cb5-94f1-cbe41ac86d89" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65f2c34a-43ea-5ba9-8212-652326193c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-221775" :question/problem "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0$ for any $n\\in N.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11({{field-6}})-({{field-7}})^{2}-28 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." :question/worked-solution "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28>0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a natural number $n$ such that \n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28>0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $n=5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11(5)-(5)^{2}-28 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e48ea1-2219-5954-8101-def7c9c2be2c" :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a8bada-43a7-5db3-86c9-88ce12798b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412496} {:answer/id #uuid "153f2e7d-0154-5a20-b6c7-0724a8b9162f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412505} {:answer/id #uuid "86f4bf6e-6328-56e1-bd5e-444e7af7a7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f4bf6e-6328-56e1-bd5e-444e7af7a7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412548} :answer-field/id #uuid "14715ea5-6dfa-5a80-a5ef-10c41c497286" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf34974-4479-59a2-b61e-4c79ee2d1bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412522} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8b5081-9d